        "id": "motion-atlas",
        "title": "presentation-motion — three visual sequences",
        "instructions": "# Motion follows the argument\n\nEach row shows three moments from an original engine study, not a slide template\nor source of business facts. Sample statistics and company names are illustrative.\n\n1. **One idea per beat:** the company study reuses a palette and sculptural motif\n   across chapters. The motif hands attention to the next title. A presentation\n   can reuse a diagram element or alignment anchor without repeating a full layout.\n2. **Editorial hierarchy:** the data study separates title, evidence and supported\n   emphasis. Establish the reading target before secondary annotations. Use\n   chart-storytelling for actual numeric encoding; never borrow sample values.\n3. **Detail and context:** the software study establishes a workspace, focuses on\n   one action and returns to its result. A training slide can isolate a component,\n   explain it, then return to the overall diagram.\n\nFor an ordered process, preserve order in timing and spatial placement. Keep\nconnectors attached to moving targets. For a comparison, establish both sides\nbefore highlighting the supported distinction. For a title slide, one precise\ntransformation and a clear reading hold can be enough. Choose from the content,\nnot from the board's row order.\n\nSources: `company`, `data-story`, `software`; see `provenance.json` for frame and\nvideo hashes. These examples demonstrate video motion, not PPTX editing support.\n",
